meta {
  name: Object Definition
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/schema/{{connectionId}}/objects/{{objectKind}}/{{objectName}}/definition
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  ~schema: public
  ~args: integer
  ~parent: orders
}

docs {
  One object's full definition, read live: { schema, name, kind, args?, parent?, definition, migration }. `definition` is null when the engine hides it (encrypted, or no VIEW DEFINITION permission). `migration` is the same definition as a re-runnable script (`CREATE OR ALTER` / `CREATE OR REPLACE`) — the starting point for an object-change write request — or null (hidden, or a PostgreSQL materialized view).

  `objectKind` is procedure | function | view | trigger. `args` picks a Postgres overload by its identity argument types (empty string = no arguments); `parent` picks a Postgres trigger by its table. Without them an ambiguous name is a 400 that lists the choices.

  Requires a valid Bearer JWT ({{token}}) plus **read** capability for the connection's environment — 403 otherwise (admins hold every environment). Each successful read is audited as OBJECT_VIEW.

  Errors: 400 for an unknown kind, an engine without objects, or an ambiguous name; 404 { error, code: "OBJECT_NOT_FOUND" }; 503 { error, code: "CONNECTION_FAILED" } when the database host is unreachable; 500 { error } for other failures.
}
